%[text] **1. Time Response** (20 marks)
%[text] a) (5 marks) You have collected some data in the lab for an electric motor. In response to a 1V unit step input, the output rotational velocity of the motor in rad/s was recorded and stored in a data file which we will not load and plot: 
load("labdataFinal20.mat")
plot(t,omega_measured) %[output:06210862]
%[text] You have to determine the approximate transfer function of the motor to match the data. Clever bunny that you are, you know that a good approximate linear model in this case is $G(s)=\\frac{K\_m}{s+\\alpha}$. By any means create a model that fits the data reasonably well and then plot step response of you model against the data to visually compare your fitted model with the data.
% --- Fit G(s) = K_m / (s + alpha) directly from data ---

% Q1a Attribute: I found this code structure at home from Copilot.
% Cost function using numeric tf
cost = @(p) sum( (omega_measured - step(tf(p(1), [1 p(2)]), t)).^2 );

% Initial guess
p0 = [omega_measured(end), 1];

% Fit parameters
p_opt = fminsearch(cost, p0);

K_m   = p_opt(1) %[output:866f18b8]
alpha = p_opt(2) %[output:7f479af0]

% Build final model
G_omega = tf(K_m, [1 alpha]) %[output:78cec4c3]
omega_guess=step(G_omega,t);
plot(t,omega_measured,t,omega_guess) %[output:7ff1d020]
