%[text]  **What can you say about the stability of the closed loop system what damping ratio’s are achievable using proportional feedback control** $D(s) = K$**?**
%[text] With proportional feedback applied to the plant G(s) = 30 / (s^2 + 4\*s + 20), the closed-loop characteristic equation becomes s^2 + 4\*s + (20 + 30\*K) = 0. For any K \>= 0, the real part of the poles is fixed at -2 and the discriminant is always negative, so the system is always stable and always underdamped. Matching this to the standard form s^2 + 2\*zeta\*wn\*s + wn^2 = 0 gives wn = sqrt(20 + 30\*K) and zeta = 2/wn. Because K \>= 0, the achievable damping ratios satisfy 0 \< zeta \<= 0.447, meaning the system can never reach critical damping or overdamping using proportional control alone. 
%[text] **What values of % overshoot are achievable using proportional feedback control** $D(s) = K$**?**
%[text] The percent overshoot for a second-order system is PO = 100\*exp(-zeta\*pi / sqrt(1 - zeta^2)), so with zeta in (0, 0.447\], the achievable overshoot ranges from about 21% (when K = 0) up to nearly 100% as K approaches infinity.
